NAME
    save - write memory to a file

SYNOPSIS
    save path addr len

DESCRIPTION
    Writes len bytes of memory from addr (both hexadecimal) to the file
    path, whose load and exec addresses become addr, and says how many
    bytes it wrote, in decimal: "saved 32 bytes". A file of that name is
    replaced; a directory of that name is not. save is a shell
    built-in.

    Errors: "usage: save path addr len (hex)", "cannot create" (the
    directory is missing, the name is not valid, or it names a
    directory), "write error".

LIMITS
    save inside a > or >> redirect (save F 5000 10 > LOG) or a pipe
    says "cannot create": the OS has one file open for writing at a
    time, and the redirect is it (see shell).
    load and run accept only files inside $5000..$CFFF: memory saved
    from elsewhere (the OS, the monitor's page) can be kept and looked
    at with cat or dump, but not loaded back. At most 65535 bytes.

EXAMPLES
    save H.BIN 5000 20      32 bytes from $5000, load and exec $5000
    save /HELLO2 5000 84    after load /BIN/HELLO: a copy that runs

NOTES
    Changed from the P8X version: the arguments are a start and a
    length (P8X: a start and an end), and the file keeps its address.
    The command is the Y1/OS shell's own (os/y1os.asm; os/y1os.c); the page is
    ported from P8X os/man/save 2026-09-23.

SEE ALSO
    load, run, dep, dump, shell
